title: A crate-pushing puzzle
intro: Sokoban with no code: a worker pushes crates onto marks, takes moves back, and a panel opens when every mark is covered.
summary: Build a Sokoban level from an empty project: a tilemap room, a grid player, crates, goals, an undo button and a Solved panel.
project: Empty | Crate Push

chapter: A room | A tilemap
create: Tilemap
rename: Level
set: Tilemap.Tiles = 11111111\n1......1\n1.11...1\n1......1\n11111111
select: Main Camera
set: Transform.Position.X = 4
set: Transform.Position.Y = -2.5
look: 4, -2.5, 4
deselect
say: A tilemap is a grid of tiles, and each number in Tiles is one: 1 is a wall and a dot is empty floor. Typing the rows is the quickest way to lay out a small level. The camera is moved over the room.
shot: room

chapter: A worker | Create Grid Player
create: Grid Player
set: Transform.Position.X = 1.5
set: Transform.Position.Y = -1.5
say: A grid player steps from cell to cell, one press for one step, and the tilemap's walls stop it.
deselect

chapter: Crates and goals | Create Crate, Create Goal
create: Crate
rename: Crate A
set: Transform.Position.X = 3.5
set: Transform.Position.Y = -1.5
deselect
create: Crate
rename: Crate B
set: Transform.Position.X = 2.5
set: Transform.Position.Y = -3.5
deselect
say: In a scene with a grid player, Create Crate makes a grid crate: a Pushable that slides one cell when the player steps into it, as long as the cell beyond is free.
deselect
create: Goal
rename: Goal A
set: Transform.Position.X = 5.5
set: Transform.Position.Y = -1.5
deselect
create: Goal
rename: Goal B
set: Transform.Position.X = 4.5
set: Transform.Position.Y = -3.5
say: A goal is a mark on the floor. It is covered while a crate rests on it, and every goal with the same All Covered Message sends it once they are all covered. That message is the level solved.
expectvalue: Goal A.Goal.All Covered Message = Solved
shot: crates

chapter: Counting | A Counter that counts goals
create: Text
rename: Crates
add: ScreenAnchor
set: ScreenAnchor.Anchor = TopLeft
set: ScreenAnchor.Offset = 3.5, -0.7
add: Counter
set: Counter.Count Goals = true
set: Counter.Format = Crates: {0} / {2}
say: A Counter can count covered goals instead of a message. {0} is how many are covered and {2} how many there are, and a Screen Anchor keeps it in the corner of the screen.
deselect

chapter: Solved | A Panel that opens by itself
create: Panel
rename: Solved
set: Panel.Open Message = Solved
create: Text
rename: Well Done
set: Text.Value = Solved!
say: A Panel stays hidden until something opens it. Its Open Message is the message that does: when the goals send Solved, it appears, and the game behind it stops. The text was made with the panel selected, so it is inside it.
deselect
shot: panel

chapter: Play it | Push, undo, solve
play
wait: 0.5
tap: Right
wait: 0.4
tap: Right
wait: 0.4
expectat: Crate A = 4.5, -1.5
tap: Right
wait: 0.5
expectat: Crate A = 5.5, -1.5
expectvalue: Crates.Text.Value = Crates: 1 / 2
say: Walk into a crate and it slides ahead of you. On its goal it turns green and the counter goes up.
tap: Z
wait: 0.4
expectat: Crate A = 4.5, -1.5
expectvalue: Crates.Text.Value = Crates: 0 / 2
say: Z takes the last move back, crate and all, and R starts the level over. The first goal you create brings a Move History along to do that.
tap: Right
wait: 0.5
tap: Left
wait: 0.3
tap: Left
wait: 0.3
tap: Left
wait: 0.3
tap: Down
wait: 0.3
tap: Down
wait: 0.3
tap: Right
wait: 0.4
tap: Right
wait: 0.5
expectat: Crate B = 4.5, -3.5
expectvalue: Crates.Text.Value = Crates: 2 / 2
wait: 0.5
shot: solved | game | 960
stop
say: Both crates are home, so the goals sent Solved and the panel opened. To make more levels, duplicate the scene and repaint the tilemap.
